NAME
       visudo - edit the sudo rules safely, with a syntax check

SYNOPSIS
       visudo [-f FILE]
       visudo -c [-q] [-f FILE]

DESCRIPTION
       visudo opens /etc/sudoers (or FILE) in an editor (vi unless $SUDO_EDITOR,
       $VISUAL or $EDITOR names another), and locks it so two people cannot
       edit it at once. When you save and leave, visudo checks the syntax of
       what you wrote. If there is an error it says where and asks what now:

           e   edit the file again (the usual answer),
           x   quit and keep the old file unchanged,
           Q   save the broken file anyway (dangerous: sudo may stop working
               for everyone).

       That check is the reason to use visudo rather than a plain editor. A
       sudoers file with a mistake can lock every administrator out of sudo;
       on a system without a root password you would then need to boot into
       a rescue shell to repair it.

       Rules for one user or group are best kept in their own file in
       /etc/sudoers.d, created with visudo -f. visudo gives such files the
       safe permissions sudo needs (0440).

OPTIONS
       -f, --file=FILE
              Edit or check FILE instead of /etc/sudoers.

       -c, --check
              Only check the syntax and report "parsed OK" (or the errors) for
              /etc/sudoers and every file it includes. Exits with 1 on an
              error.

       -q, --quiet
              With -c: print nothing; only the exit status tells.

       -s, --strict
              Also report things that are allowed but probably mistakes, such
              as an alias used before it is defined.

EXAMPLES
       Let the group admins run any command as root, in a file of its own:

           visudo -f /etc/sudoers.d/admins
           (type the line:  %admins ALL=(ALL) ALL  then save with :wq)

       Write such a file in one step and check it:

           echo '%admins ALL=(ALL) ALL' > /etc/sudoers.d/admins
           visudo -c

       Let student restart httpd without a password:

           echo 'student ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl restart httpd' \
               > /etc/sudoers.d/student
           visudo -cf /etc/sudoers.d/student

NOTES
       Files in /etc/sudoers.d whose names contain a dot or end in ~ are
       ignored, so call the file admins, not admins.conf.

FILES
       /etc/sudoers
              The main rules (includes /etc/sudoers.d).

       /etc/sudoers.d/
              Extra rule files.

SEE ALSO
       sudoers(5), sudo(8), vipw(8)
